 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Provides for state recognition and acknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians; provides that the Montaukett Indians shall have a chief or sachem, three tribal trustees and a tribal secretary; further provides for the qualification of voters; makes related provisions.",
 "title": "Relates to the state recognition and acknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A4069",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A4069-2021",
     "printNo": "A4069",
     "session": 2021,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 110\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 4069, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the Indian law, in relation to the state recogni-\n      tion and acknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  Currently, in New York there are nine governmentally recognized Indian\nNations or Tribes. This bill would  provide  State  recognition  to  the\nMontaukett  Indians.  To  be recognized as an Indian tribe under federal\nlaw, an entity must show a continued history going back over  a  century\nwith respect to several categories. Similarly, New York has historically\nonly  recognized  Tribes  and Nations that have had a continuous govern-\nment-to-government relationship with  New  York  since  this  State  was\nestablished.  Due  to  these historic underpinnings, there is no general\nstatutory provision for the new recognition of Indian Nations or Tribes.\n \n  Recognition requires the determination that a sovereign  nation,  with\nits  own  functioning  government, has continually existed, and as such,\nrequires due diligence on the part of the State to collect  and  analyze\nevidence to ensure that any entity requesting recognition is, in fact, a\ngovernment that has continued in existence over the past centuries.\n \n  In  connection  with  a 2013 veto of a similar bill, the Department of\nState was directed to create a process whereby a  group  of  individuals\ncould submit documentary evidence to show that they have had a continued\nexistence.  Since  2018,  the Department of State received documentation\nfrom two different groups of  individuals,  both  declaring  to  be  the\nMontaukett  Indians.  While both groups have begun to participate in the\nacknowledgement process, to date, neither group has provided the Depart-\nment of State with necessary  information  requested  from  each  group.\nThis  bill  would  have  the Department of State bypass its thorough and\ncareful consideration and instead grant State recognition to one of  the\ngroups petitioning for recognition.\n \n  Vetoing  this  bill  does  not  preclude with Montaukett heritage from\ncontinuing to practice their customs  and  traditions  and  teach  their\nprogeny  the  values  of their culture, or from supplying the persistent\ninformation requested by the  Department  of  State.  My  administration\nurges  all individuals who believe they should be formally recognized as\nMontaukett Indians to continue to work with the Department of State  and\nprovide the necessary documentation through the existing process.\n \n  Based on the foregoing, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 110,
